Both constants follow, with Design V retaining the additional hypotheses in the notes. The main correction is that rigorous **batch** smoothness testing suffices; a rigorous \(L^{o(1)}\) test for each isolated candidate is unnecessary. Throughout, algorithms are classical, \(L=\exp\sqrt{\log N\log\log N}\), \(B=L^a\), and \(h=N^{\theta+o(1)}\), with fixed \(\theta>0\).

**Task 1: the consequences are proved implications.**

The smoothness assumption gives acceptance probability
\[
p_B\ge L^{-\theta/(2a)-o(1)},
\]
because \(\log\rho(\log h/\log B)=-(\theta/(2a)+o(1))\log L\).

Dixon needs \(L^{a+o(1)}\) accepted rows. Relation collection and sparse linear algebra therefore cost
\[
L^{a+\theta/(2a)+o(1)},\qquad L^{2a+o(1)}.
\]
Minimizing their maximum gives
\[
a=\sqrt{\theta/2},\qquad \boxed{c_D=\sqrt{2\theta}}.
\]
No rank hypothesis is needed: more rows than columns force a dependency.

For fruitfulness, handle nonunits by gcd and perfect powers separately. Put \(q=|\mu_2|=2^{\omega(N)}\), so \(q\ge4\). Conditional on its square, every root has probability at least \(1/(\kappa q)\). Fix all other roots in a dependency containing a sampler row. Exactly two choices are bad, hence
\[
\Pr(\text{proper factor})\ge\frac{q-2}{\kappa q}\ge\frac1{2\kappa}.
\]
Dependency selection must inspect squares and certificates only. This costs \(L^{o(1)}\) repetitions; there is no \(\kappa^{\#\text{rows}}\) loss.

For Design V, write \(\delta=3/(d+1)\). Under the **unproved LV supply transfer, genuine-certificate RK, and effective LV §6 postprocessing preserving a nonzero sampler projection**, the three exponents are
\[
2a,\qquad a+\frac{\delta}{2a},\qquad\frac{\theta}{2a}.
\]
RK deficiency \(r=L^{o(1)}\) requires a batch of \(r+1\) sampler rows, rather than a claim that one target individually lands in the span. Taking \(a=\sqrt\theta/2\) gives
\[
\boxed{c_V=\sqrt\theta},\qquad
\boxed{\delta\le\theta/2}.
\]
Thus \(d+1\ge6/\theta\), with \(d\) fixed and odd. For \(\theta=2/3\), \(d\ge9\) suffices. Raw matrix membership alone is insufficient, as [hypD_results.md §5](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/hypD_results.md:545) correctly specifies.

If each sampler call costs \(L^{s+o(1)}\), the proved optimization becomes
\[
\boxed{c_D(s)=s+\sqrt{2\theta}},
\]
and, conditionally for Design V,
\[
c_V(s)=\min_{a>0}\max\left\{2a,\ a+\frac{\delta}{2a},\
s+\frac{\theta}{2a}\right\}.
\]
When the LV term does not bind,
\[
\boxed{c_V(s)=\frac{s+\sqrt{s^2+4\theta}}2},
\quad \delta\le c_V(s)^2/2.
\]
Consequently Dixon beats \(1\) when \(s+\sqrt{2\theta}<1\); Design V does so when \(s+\theta<1\), subject to its hypotheses.

The hidden losses can be controlled rigorously:

- Choose an explicit preprocessing cutoff \(y_0=\exp\sqrt{\log N}\). Trial division costs \(L^{o(1)}\), and afterwards \(\omega(N)\le\sqrt{\log N}\), giving \(2^\omega=L^{o(1)}\). A vaguely specified \(L^{o(1)}\) cutoff alone would not establish this.
- Vallée’s original counting theorem assumes prime factors exceeding \(L\). Pollard–Strassen removes factors up to \(L\) in \(L^{1/2+o(1)}\), within both constants at \(\theta=2/3\). A hypothetical smaller-\(\theta\) theorem should not inherit that stronger cutoff unnecessarily.
- Bernstein’s deterministic batch algorithm identifies smooth parts, and his related algorithms recover the factorizations, in time essentially linear in the total input length. Batches of at least \(B\) candidates make the amortized cost \(L^{o(1)}\). Such batches fit both optimizations. Thus no ECM smoothness hypothesis is required here. [D. J. Bernstein, *How to find smooth parts of integers*, 10 May 2004, Theorems 2.2–2.3](https://nymity.ch/anomalous-tor-keys/bibliography/pdf/Bernstein04.pdf).

**Smoothness counting in square classes does not introduce a fixed extra constant.** Here is the product argument, including multiplicity.

Set \(Y=\sqrt h\). Standard smooth-number estimates, with a weighted Rankin bound discarding excessive numbers of prime factors, give a set \(S\) of coprime \(B\)-smooth integers at most \(Y\) such that
\[
|S|=Y L^{-\theta/(4a)+o(1)},\qquad
\Omega(u)\le K=\sqrt{\log N}(\log\log N)^{1/4}=o(\log L).
\]
Excluding primes dividing the preprocessed \(N\) preserves this estimate. The underlying smooth-number lower bound is [Canfield–Erdős–Pomerance, *J. Number Theory* **17** (1983), 1–28](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper39.pdf).

Partition \(S\) into its \(q\) square classes modulo \(N\). At least \(|S|^2/q\) ordered pairs have the same class; their products are squares modulo \(N\), smooth, and at most \(h\). Each product has at most
\[
\tau(uv)\le2^{2K}=L^{o(1)}
\]
representations. Hence there are at least
\[
hL^{-\theta/(2a)-o(1)}
\]
distinct such products. This proves the required lower bound under quasi-uniform sampling over the interval’s square classes. Using merely the unrestricted bound \(\tau(n)=N^{o(1)}\) would be too weak. **Fibre symmetry by itself supplies no smoothness law**; that remains a separate sampler requirement.

**Task 2: \(2/3\) is Vallée’s curvature–density threshold.**

Writing \(x=x_1+t\), the exact condition is
\[
s\equiv x_1^2+2x_1t\pmod N,\qquad |s+t^2|\le h.
\]
The affine lattice has determinant \(N\). On a tangent cell \(|t|\le T\), absorbing the quadratic remainder within width \(O(h)\) requires \(T=O(\sqrt h)\). Its largest useful area is therefore
\[
Th=O(h^{3/2}).
\]
The determinant scale is reached when \(h^{3/2}\gtrsim N\), namely \(\theta\ge2/3\).

Vallée’s actual proof retains the two parabolas and uses variable Farey intervals, rather than assuming every tangent rectangle behaves regularly. Near \(aN/(2q)\), a basis includes
\[
(q,\,2qx_1-aN);
\]
lattice points lie on nearly horizontal lines with horizontal spacing \(q\) and transverse spacing \(N/q\). Continued fractions locate the interval. Line counts in the “legs,” and exact counts on the bounded number of exceptional “chest/feet” lines, give sampling weights.

With Farey order \(K\asymp N/h\), the largest denominators reach the curvature scale precisely when \(K\lesssim\sqrt h\). Below it, the uniform lower counts fail. Vallée explicitly discusses this failure in §5.1. [B. Vallée, *Generation of elements with small modular squares and provably fast integer factoring algorithms*, **Math. Comp. 56** (1991), 823–849, Theorem 5 and §5.1](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/253163661_Generation_of_elements_with_small_modular_squares_and_provably_fast_integer_factoring_algorithms).

**Task 3(a): the proposed rank-one collapse is proved, with a specific scope.**

For \(x=x_1+\sum_iw_it_i\), the quadratic remainder is
\[
